{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Of all flesh, etc.</b> It is well known that the animals are extremely numerous, and among them are very large ones — elephants and re'em and others — and the creeping things that creep upon the earth are exceedingly many; and also among the birds of the heavens the species are beyond counting. As our Sages said (Hullin 63a), there are a hundred and twenty species of unclean birds in the east, and they are all a species of kite, while the clean birds are beyond number. Now he needs to bring from all of them, so that they may reproduce as their kinds, and when you gather food for all of them that shall be eaten for a full year, this ark will not contain them — nor even ten like it. Rather, it is a miracle that the little held the much. And if you should say, let him make it small and rely on this miracle — it was the will of HaShem, may He be blessed, to make it large, so that the people of his generation would see it and wonder at it, and speak of it, and talk about the matter of the Flood and the gathering of the beast and the animal and the bird into it; perhaps they would repent. Moreover, they made it large to minimize the miracle, for such is the way in all the miracles that are in the Torah or in the Prophets: to do what is within human power to do, and the remainder will be in the hands of Heaven. And do not be misled to say (see Ibn Ezra below, verse 16) that the cubits were three hundred cubits by the cubits of Noah, and that he was large — for if so, the people too were large, and likewise the animals and the birds in those generations were large, until the world was punished by the Flood — and furthermore, the cubits of the Torah are the cubits of the Torah." "<b>You shall bring into the ark to keep alive with you.</b> He commanded him to engage and help them himself in bringing them into the ark, and to exert effort on their behalf so that they live, just as he would exert effort on his own life."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כַּפִּילִים וְכָרֵאמִים' refers to elephants (פילים) and the re'em, a large animal mentioned in biblical and rabbinic literature."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:01.821622164Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}